\begin{abstract}
We propose a two-stage method for estimating univariate probability densities using a small mixture of Gaussian components with different locations and bandwidths. The first stage selects components through nonnegative, sparsity-penalized moment matching, with discrepancies weighted by their estimated covariance. The second stage refits the selected components under nonnegativity and unit-sum constraints, ensuring that the resulting estimate is a valid probability density. The method uses the same Gaussian functions to represent the density and measure its agreement with the observed data. We show that this choice connects the population moment residual directly to the gradient of the integrated squared error, providing a mathematical basis for the measurement design. Experiments on 15 Marron–Wand benchmark densities, with sample sizes ranging from 200 to 5,000, evaluate estimation accuracy, computational cost, and model compactness. At 5,000 observations, the proposed method evaluates the fitted density approximately 127 times faster than the Sheather–Jones kernel density estimator, using a model approximately 89 times smaller under the tested implementation. This computational advantage comes with an accuracy trade-off: its paired geometric-mean integrated squared error is 2.6–4.9 times that of the Sheather–Jones estimator and 7–23\% higher than that of a density-valid SPADES adaptation. Occasional large estimation errors and incomplete optimization convergence remain limitations. A controlled follow-up experiment on a family of two-component mixtures whose narrow component is 1 to 8 times narrower than the broad one tests whether the multiscale dictionary itself helps: it does not. A single-scale ablation with one cross-validated bandwidth on the same centres attains lower integrated squared error in seven of eight target--sample-size cells (paired geometric-mean ratios of 1.9--13.8) and is inconclusive in the eighth, a size-matched single-scale control shows the atom count is not the cause, and the widest dictionary scale is never selected; density-valid SPADES behaves the same way on the same dictionary. These findings demonstrate the method’s potential for applications requiring compact density representations and repeated density evaluation, locate its accuracy deficit in the dictionary-plus-sparse-selection design rather than in the moment criterion alone, and identify opportunities to improve accuracy and numerical reliability.

\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} density estimation, method of moments, sparse
mixture models, measurement design, kernel density estimation.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Nonparametric density estimation underpins classification, clustering,
anomaly detection and level-set estimation \cite{sheather1991,botev2010}. Two
broad families dominate practice. Kernel density estimators place a kernel at
every observation; they are simple, accurate and essentially free to fit, but
the fitted model \emph{is} the sample, so memory and query cost grow linearly
with $N$. Sparse mixture estimators instead select a small number of
components from a dictionary; they pay a fitting cost once and then evaluate
in time independent of $N$. SPADES \cite{spades,bunea2007} is the canonical
$\ell_1$-penalised member of this family, minimising an empirical
integrated-square loss over a fixed dictionary.

This paper concerns a moment-based member of that family. Instead of an
integrated-square loss, the density is fitted by matching a vector of
\emph{measurement functionals}, integrals of the density against a bank of
kernels, to their empirical counterparts, with a covariance weighting in the
spirit of the generalised method of moments \cite{hansen1982,hall2005}. The
construction is attractive for three reasons: the measurement family need not
coincide with the representation dictionary, each stage is a convex quadratic
program, and the output is a genuine probability density.

The estimator as previously configured did not perform well. Our contribution
is to identify why, to correct it, and to report the corrected estimator's
strengths and remaining weaknesses under a locked evaluation.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{A matched measurement design for sparse density estimation.}
We use the same Gaussian functions for moment measurement and density
representation, aligning the measurement family with the dictionary's
locations and bandwidths. This construction connects the population moment
residual directly to the gradient of the integrated squared error, providing
a mathematical basis for the design. 
%Experiments on $15$ Marron--Wand
% benchmark densities at $N = 200$, $1000$, and $5000$ evaluate its accuracy
% and its sensitivity to the choice of measurement family
% (Section~\ref{sec:results}, Figure~\ref{fig:accuracy}).
\item \textbf{A mathematical characterization of matched moment fitting.}
We establish the connection between moment matching and integrated squared
error: when $k_j = b_j$, the design matrix equals the dictionary's Gram
matrix, and the population moment residual equals one half of the exact
risk gradient (Proposition~\ref{prop:matched}). We further characterize
the conditions under which the weighting matrix affects the unconstrained
moment-fitting solution, supported by an analytic counterexample
(Proposition~\ref{prop:omega}).
\item \textbf{Oracle analysis of residual estimation error.}
We use population moments and the population covariance to assess the
error that remains after removing sampling noise from the measured
moments, conditional on the sample-built dictionary. Under the matched
measurement design, the population-moment fit retains an excess above
the dictionary's minimum achievable ISE corresponding to approximately
$0.001$--$17\%$ of the total estimation error in the tested conditions.
This analysis helps distinguish moment-sampling effects from residual
effects of the fitting criterion, constraints, and numerical optimisation
(Section~\ref{sec:mechanism}, Figure~\ref{fig:mechanism}).
\item \textbf{Compact density representations with fast evaluation.}
The proposed estimator represents the fitted density using a small
Gaussian mixture, reducing storage requirements and evaluation time.
At $N=5000$, it evaluates the density in $1.17\,\mu$s per query point,
compared with $147.8\,\mu$s for the Sheather--Jones estimator, and uses
a serialised model of $1{,}344$ bytes compared with $120{,}000$ bytes.
These measurements, obtained under identical query conditions,
demonstrate approximately $127$-fold faster evaluation and $89$-fold
lower model storage under the tested implementation
(Section~\ref{sec:cost}, Figure~\ref{fig:cost}).
\item \textbf{A systematic assessment of accuracy and numerical stability.}
We evaluate the proposed estimator against conventional kernel estimators
and a density-valid SPADES adaptation, and examine its sensitivity to
optimisation accuracy and the iteration budget. The results identify an
accuracy disadvantage relative to these baselines and a $2.7\%$ rate of
catastrophic replicates at $N=5000$, as defined in
Section~\ref{sec:results}. On the diagnostic subset, first-stage
optimisation reaches its stated tolerance in $13.5\%$ of instances,
and the selected support varies with the iteration budget.
Feasible-perturbation experiments further demonstrate that moment
discrepancy can remain unchanged or decrease while ISE increases
by an amount comparable to the fitted error. These findings clarify
the method's practical limitations and identify priorities for improving
its reliability
(Sections~\ref{sec:results}--\ref{sec:limitations}).
\item \textbf{A controlled test of multiscale bandwidth selection.}
On a locked family of mixtures with a narrow component $1$ to $8$ times
narrower than the broad one ($160$ datasets, six methods, $960$ fits), a
single-scale ablation of the estimator with one cross-validated bandwidth on
the same $60$ centres was more accurate than the multiscale dictionary in
seven of eight cells and inconclusive in the eighth; a size-matched
single-scale control (300 centres) was better than the multiscale entry on all
$40$ of its paired datasets, the widest dictionary scale received zero weight
in every fit, and the SPADES adaptation used the same dictionary the same way.
Multiscale selection is not what makes the estimator useful; compactness is
(Section~\ref{sec:bandwidth}).
\end{enumerate}
