\section{Related work}
\label{sec:related}

The proposed method connects three lines of research: sparse
dictionary-based density estimation, moment matching with kernel
representations, and compact probability models for efficient evaluation.

\paragraph{Sparse mixture and minimum-distance density estimation.}
Sparse density estimators approximate an unknown distribution using a
small number of components selected from a larger dictionary. SPADES
\cite{spades} provides a framework for this approach by combining an
empirical integrated-square loss with an $\ell_1$ penalty. Its objective
has the form
\[
    w^\top G w - 2s^\top w
    + \sum_{i=1}^{M}\gamma_i |w_i|,
\]
where $G$ is the dictionary Gram matrix, $s$ contains empirical averages
of the basis functions, and $\gamma_i$ are penalty weights. This
formulation encourages sparse representations and admits statistical
guarantees under appropriate assumptions. Hong et al.\
\cite{hong2013} also investigate sparse density estimation through
integrated-square-error minimisation, using sequential kernel selection
to construct compact estimates.

The proposed estimator shares the objective of selecting a parsimonious
mixture, but fits its coefficients through covariance-weighted moment
matching. Its second stage enforces nonnegativity and unit total mass
on the selected components. Although both approaches involve quadratic
optimisation, the moment-based refit generally differs from minimising
the empirical integrated-square loss. A density-valid adaptation of
SPADES therefore provides a closely related comparator for examining
the effect of the fitting criterion on a common dictionary.

\paragraph{Moment matching and kernel representations.}
Estimation through moment conditions is a well-established alternative
to likelihood-based inference. The generalised method of moments
provides a framework for combining empirical moment conditions with
a weighting matrix that accounts for their variability and dependence
\cite{hansen1982,hall2005,chamberlain1987}. Its statistical properties
depend on the specification of the moment conditions, the weighting
scheme, and the assumptions governing estimation.

Kernel representations extend this perspective by expressing
distributional information through expectations of functions.
Song et al.\ \cite{song2008} estimate mixtures by matching probability
distributions in a reproducing kernel Hilbert space. For fixed
components, the resulting discrepancy yields a quadratic objective
in the mixture weights; Gaussian components and Gaussian kernels
allow the required expectations to be evaluated analytically.
More recently, the kernel method of moments
\cite{kremer2023} uses maximum mean discrepancy within a framework
for estimation under moment restrictions, allowing candidate
distributions beyond reweightings of the observed samples.
Compressive statistical learning
\cite{gribonval2020} instead summarises data through a finite vector
of empirical random-feature moments and fits models using that
representation.

Our method uses a finite collection of kernel measurements together
with an estimated covariance weighting and a separate dictionary
for density representation. We focus on how the relationship between
these two families affects estimation. In particular, choosing the
measurement functions to coincide with the dictionary atoms connects
the population moment residual to the gradient of the integrated
squared error (Proposition~\ref{prop:matched}). This identity provides
an interpretation of the matched design, while the experiments
assess its practical behaviour under sparsity selection and
probability constraints.

\paragraph{Compact density representations and efficient evaluation.}
Reducing the storage and evaluation costs of kernel estimators is
another motivation for sparse density models. Kernel thinning
\cite{dwivedi2024} selects representative observations while controlling
discrepancy in a kernel function space. Low-rank thinning
\cite{carrell2025} develops this direction by exploiting approximate
low-rank structure in kernel or data matrices. Coreset density
estimation \cite{turner2021} studies how a reduced subset of observations
can retain statistical accuracy while providing a smaller
representation.

Positive semidefinite (PSD) probability models
\cite{rudi2021} offer a complementary approach. They represent
nonnegative functions through PSD quadratic forms in kernel features,
with theoretical approximation guarantees and tractable operations
such as integration and marginalisation. Their representation differs
from both a selected subset of observations and a sparse mixture
with nonnegative scalar weights.

The proposed estimator pursues compactness by selecting Gaussian
components from a multiscale location--scale dictionary. This permits
component locations and bandwidths to be chosen from the dictionary
rather than requiring one component per observation. We therefore
evaluate compactness through model storage and density-evaluation
time, alongside estimation error and fitting cost. These measures
characterise the practical trade-off without treating sparsity alone
as evidence of computational superiority.

\paragraph{Benchmarking and statistical evaluation.}
The Marron--Wand normal-mixture family \cite{marron1992} provides
a reproducible collection of density shapes for assessing
nonparametric estimators. We use its $15$ targets as a fixed benchmark,
with common datasets across methods to support paired comparisons.
Following recommendations for simulation studies
\cite{morris2019}, we distinguish variability across independently
generated datasets from uncertainty in estimated performance
summaries. Results include estimation accuracy, computational cost,
model size, and numerical diagnostics, providing a joint assessment
of statistical performance and practical reliability.
