\section{Experimental setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\subsection{Benchmark densities and sample sizes}

We evaluated the proposed method on the $15$ Marron--Wand
Gaussian-mixture densities \cite{marron1992}. These targets,
denoted by $f_0,\ldots,f_{14}$, represent different density
shapes, including symmetric, skewed, multimodal, and
narrow-peaked distributions. Their parameters are provided
in Supplement~S2.

For each target, we considered sample sizes
$N\in\{200,1000,5000\}$ and generated $R=30$ independent
datasets. Six estimators were fitted to each dataset,
giving
\[
15\times3\times30\times6=8{,}100
\]
final fits. Within each replication, all methods received
the same observations. This paired design reduces the
influence of differences between generated datasets when
comparing methods.

\subsection{Compared methods}

The comparison included:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{AKDE-M:} the proposed estimator with matched
measurement functions and dictionary atoms.
\item \textbf{AKDE-V2:} a measurement-design ablation using
a separate Gaussian measurement bank.
\item \textbf{SPADES-simplex:} a density-valid adaptation
of SPADES, combining nonnegative penalised selection
under the empirical integrated-square loss with a
simplex-constrained refit.
\item \textbf{Sheather--Jones KDE:} a kernel density
estimator using the Sheather--Jones bandwidth selector
\cite{sheather1991}.
\item \textbf{Abramson KDE:} an adaptive kernel density
estimator with locally varying bandwidths
\cite{abramson1982}.
\item \textbf{Gaussian mixture:} a mixture fitted by the
expectation--maximisation algorithm \cite{dempster1977},
with its component count selected by validation.
\end{enumerate}

All dictionary-based methods used the same dictionary
of $60$ locations and $5$ bandwidths, giving $300$
candidate Gaussian components. Holding the dictionary
fixed makes comparisons between their fitting
procedures more interpretable.

\subsection{Hyperparameter selection}

Parameters requiring validation were selected using
three-fold cross-validation and a held-out
integrated-square score. For a fitted density
$\widehat f_{\mathrm{tr}}$ and validation observations
$x_1^{\mathrm{val}},\ldots,x_{n_{\mathrm{val}}}^{\mathrm{val}}$,
this score is
\[
J_{\mathrm{val}}
=
\int \widehat f_{\mathrm{tr}}(x)^2\,dx
-
\frac{2}{n_{\mathrm{val}}}
\sum_{i=1}^{n_{\mathrm{val}}}
\widehat f_{\mathrm{tr}}(x_i^{\mathrm{val}}).
\]
Smaller values indicate better validation performance.

For AKDE, the sparsity parameter was evaluated on a
six-point geometric path with relative values from
$3\times10^{-4}$ to $0.7$, and the covariance
regularisation parameter was chosen from
\[
\rho\in\{10^{-6},10^{-3},10^{-1},1\}.
\]
The support threshold was fixed at $\tau=10^{-3}$.

All data-dependent quantities were constructed from
the training observations within each fold. The true
density was used only to evaluate estimation error
and to conduct the explicitly identified oracle
diagnostics in Section~\ref{sec:mechanism}.

The confirmation configurations and replication count
were fixed before generating the confirmation datasets.
Development and confirmation used separate seed sets,
and the configuration was not retuned after inspecting
confirmation results.

\subsection{Accuracy and variability}

The primary accuracy measure was integrated squared
error:
\[
E_{j,d,N,r}
=
\int
\left\{
\widehat f_{j,d,N,r}(x)-f_d(x)
\right\}^2\,dx,
\]
where $j$ identifies the method, $d$ the target density,
$N$ the sample size, and $r$ the replication.
The Gaussian-mixture targets permit analytical
evaluation of this integral.

For each method, target, and sample size, we report
the arithmetic mean and sample standard deviation
across the $R=30$ independent datasets:
\[
\overline E_{j,d,N}
=
\frac{1}{R}\sum_{r=1}^{R}E_{j,d,N,r},
\qquad
s_{j,d,N}
=
\sqrt{
\frac{1}{R-1}
\sum_{r=1}^{R}
\left(E_{j,d,N,r}-\overline E_{j,d,N}\right)^2
}.
\]
Table entries are expressed as
$\overline E_{j,d,N}\pm s_{j,d,N}$.
The mean describes average error, while the standard
deviation describes variation between independently
generated datasets. A smaller mean indicates better
accuracy; a smaller standard deviation indicates
more stable performance.

For the overall benchmark table, we summarise all
$K=15R=450$ fits per method and sample size:
\[
\overline E_{j,N}
=
\frac{1}{15R}
\sum_{d=0}^{14}\sum_{r=1}^{R}E_{j,d,N,r},
\]
\[
s^{\mathrm{pool}}_{j,N}
=
\sqrt{
\frac{1}{15R-1}
\sum_{d=0}^{14}\sum_{r=1}^{R}
\left(E_{j,d,N,r}-\overline E_{j,N}\right)^2
}.
\]
Because all targets have the same number of
replications, this mean gives each target equal
weight. The pooled SD includes both differences
between target densities and variation between
datasets. It is not a confidence interval or the
standard error of the benchmark mean. Per-target
mean~$\pm$~SD tables are therefore provided separately
in the supplementary material.

Medians are retained as additional summaries because
a small number of large errors can substantially
increase the mean and SD.

\paragraph{Paired comparisons.}
We additionally compare methods through their
within-replication ISE ratios. The benchmark
geometric-mean ratio between methods $j$ and $k$ is
\[
Q_{j/k,N}
=
\exp\left\{
\frac{1}{15R}
\sum_{d=0}^{14}\sum_{r=1}^{R}
\log\left(
\frac{E_{j,d,N,r}}{E_{k,d,N,r}}
\right)
\right\}.
\]
A value below one favours method $j$, while a value
above one favours method $k$. This statistic is
distinct from both an arithmetic mean of ratios
and a ratio of arithmetic means.

Bootstrap uncertainty analyses use $10{,}000$
resamples of replication IDs within each target
and sample size, preserving pairing between methods.
These analyses are reported separately from the
mean~$\pm$~SD tables.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Density validity and numerical completion}

All $8{,}100$ fits completed without execution errors
and returned valid probability densities. The maximum
reported deviation of total probability mass from
one was $8.9\times10^{-16}$. Neither the zero-solution
fallback nor the support cap was activated.

Successful execution and density validity do not
imply that an optimisation problem was solved to its
requested tolerance. In the confirmation study,
the first-stage solver reached a relative
Karush--Kuhn--Tucker (KKT) tolerance of $10^{-8}$ in
$5.6\%$ of AKDE-M fits, $0.6\%$ of AKDE-V2 fits,
and $28.1\%$ of SPADES-simplex fits.
These numerical limitations are examined in
Section~\ref{sec:limitations}.

\subsection{Estimation accuracy}

