\paragraph{Effect of matching the measurement functions.}
AKDE-M achieved benchmark paired geometric-mean
ISE ratios of $0.453$, $0.174$, and $0.0822$
relative to the measurement-design ablation at
$N=200$, $1000$, and $5000$, respectively.
These correspond to reductions of approximately
$55\%$, $83\%$, and $92\%$ on this ratio scale.
The per-target geometric-mean ratio favoured
AKDE-M on all $15$ densities at every sample size.

The pooled median ISE of AKDE-M was $0.0611$,
$0.0136$, and approximately $0.00317$ as sample
size increased. Its median error therefore
decreased by approximately a factor of $19.3$
between $N=200$ and $N=5000$. This demonstrates
a substantial improvement over the observed
sample-size range, but does not establish an
asymptotic convergence rate.

\paragraph{Comparison with conventional estimators.}
AKDE-M remained less accurate than the strongest
comparison methods. Relative to SPADES-simplex,
its benchmark paired geometric-mean ISE ratios
were approximately $1.23$, $1.07$, and $1.15$.
Thus, the proposed method incurred a $7$--$23\%$
error disadvantage on this measure.

Relative to Sheather--Jones KDE, the paired
geometric-mean ISE was $2.6$--$4.9$ times larger;
the corresponding ratio of benchmark arithmetic
means was $2.2$--$4.5$. These summaries describe
different aspects of performance and are not
interchangeable.

The results show that matching the measurement
functions improves the moment-based estimator,
but does not establish an accuracy advantage
over integrated-square or conventional kernel
density fitting.

\subsection{Variability and large-error replications}

To examine unusually poor fits, we classified
a replication as a large-error case when its ISE
exceeded five times the median ISE for the same
method, target density, and sample size.

For AKDE-M, the reported frequencies were
$0.22\%$, $0\%$, and $2.67\%$ at $N=200$,
$1000$, and $5000$. The most extreme case had
an ISE approximately $169$ times its cell median.
No such cases were reported for SPADES-simplex
or Sheather--Jones KDE in this experiment.

These observations explain why median performance
alone is insufficient to assess reliability.
A method can perform well on most datasets while
occasionally producing a much larger error,
which increases its mean and standard deviation.

At $N=5000$, $11$ of the $12$ large-error AKDE-M
fits selected the smallest candidate sparsity
parameter. These fits also had substantially
larger first-stage KKT residuals. This association
suggests that weak penalisation and incomplete
optimisation warrant further investigation,
but does not establish either as the sole cause.

The smallest candidate $\lambda$ was selected
in $22.7$--$57.1\%$ of AKDE-M fits, depending on
sample size. Frequent selection at a grid boundary
motivates a sensitivity analysis of the tuning
range. It does not, by itself, show that extending
the grid toward still weaker penalisation would
improve performance.

