\section{Does multiscale bandwidth selection help?}
\label{sec:bandwidth}

The dictionary of Section~\ref{sec:method} offers every centre at five
bandwidths. This section asks, in a controlled follow-up experiment, whether
that freedom is what makes the estimator useful. All numbers in this section
are generated from the saved per-fit results of the experiment package
(\texttt{experiments/bandwidth\_flexibility}); the protocol was written and
hashed before the first dataset was drawn. It is a prospective follow-up
protocol, not a preregistration: the question and the target family were
chosen with the results of Section~\ref{sec:results} in hand.

\subsection{Why multiscale selection might help, and who else has it}

A density with a broad body and a sharp feature cannot be represented
economically by atoms of one width: wide atoms smear the feature, narrow atoms
need many terms to build the body and add variance. A dictionary that offers
both lets a sparse selector place a few narrow atoms on the feature and a few
wide ones under the body, which is the intuition behind location--scale
dictionaries in sparse density estimation. Bandwidth flexibility is not,
however, unique to the proposed estimator. The Abramson estimator varies its
bandwidth per sample point, a Gaussian mixture fitted by EM has a free variance
per component, and the SPADES adaptation selects from the same multiscale
dictionary. Among the methods compared here only the Sheather--Jones estimator
and the single-scale ablation introduced below use one bandwidth. Three
questions are therefore kept apart: whether multiscale AKDE beats a
single-scale AKDE (representation), whether AKDE uses the common dictionary
at least as well as SPADES-simplex (fitting procedure), and whether AKDE's
accuracy, size and query cost form a useful operating point against the
kernel and mixture comparators (practical trade-off).

\subsection{Controlled setup}

\paragraph{Targets.} We used the family
\[
f_r(x) = 0.75\,\mathcal N(x;0,1) + 0.25\,\mathcal N(x;2,r^{-2}),
\qquad r\in\{1,2,4,8\},
\]
where the third argument is a variance, so the narrow component has standard
deviation $1/r$ and $r$ indexes scale heterogeneity: at $r=1$ both components
have the same width, at $r=8$ the second is eight times narrower. The narrow
region $A_r=[2-2/r,\,2+2/r]$ was declared before any fit was run. Regional
errors are integrals of $(\widehat f - f_r)^2$ over $A_r$ and over its
complement, against the full target, computed in closed form through the
Gaussian product identity and Gaussian distribution functions; the identity
$\mathrm{ISE} = \mathrm{ISE}_{A_r} + \mathrm{ISE}_{\mathbb R\setminus A_r}$
holds to a relative residual below $10^{-10}$ on every fit. Because $A_r$
shrinks with $r$, regional errors are compared between methods within an $r$,
never across $r$. This family is a targeted test of scale heterogeneity, not a
representative sample of densities; the 15-target benchmark of
Section~\ref{sec:results} remains the broad assessment.

\paragraph{Design.} $N\in\{500,5000\}$ and $R=20$ independent datasets per
target and sample size give $160$ datasets. Six methods were fitted to every
dataset ($960$ final fits), with identical observations and identical
three-fold validation splits for every method on a dataset. Seeds came from a
namespace disjoint from the development and confirmation namespaces of
Section~\ref{sec:setup}; development checks used a further disjoint namespace
and were never used to change the method or the protocol. $R$ was fixed in
advance and not increased after inspecting results.

\paragraph{Methods.} (B) \emph{Multiscale AKDE} is AKDE-M exactly as
confirmed in Section~\ref{sec:results}: $60$ centres on
$[\min x - 4h,\ \max x + 4h]$ at bandwidths
$\{0.05, 0.15, 0.45, 1.34, 4\}\times h$, with $h$ the Silverman reference
bandwidth of the training sample, matched measurements ($m=M=300$), and the
tuning grids of Section~\ref{sec:setup}. (A) \emph{Single-scale AKDE} keeps
the same $60$ centres and the same two-stage construction, measurements and
tuning criterion, but gives all atoms one shared bandwidth per candidate fit,
chosen by the same three-fold validation score from nine geometrically spaced
values on $[0.05h,\,4h]$ that contain the five multiscale bandwidths; its
measurement family is matched to its own $60$-atom dictionary. Its search is
$216$ candidates per fold against $24$ for the multiscale entry, which cost
$\bfSsTuneCostRatioNSmall\times$ and $\bfSsTuneCostRatioNLarge\times$ the
multiscale tuning time at $N=500$ and $5000$. The true density was never used
to select a bandwidth. (E) \emph{SPADES-simplex} uses exactly the dictionary of
(B): on every dataset the SHA-256 of the two dictionaries' centre and variance
arrays agree and every fitted atom of both methods lies in that dictionary.
(C) Sheather--Jones KDE, (D) Abramson KDE and (F) the EM Gaussian mixture with
component-specific variances and validation-selected component count are the
implementations of Section~\ref{sec:setup}. Reference bandwidths and
dictionaries are rebuilt from the training part of each fold and from the full
sample for the final fit.

\paragraph{Controls.} Two further AKDE variants, locked before their results
were examined, were run on $r\in\{1,8\}$, $N\in\{500,5000\}$ and the first ten
replication seeds ($40$ datasets, $80$ fits), paired with the main-run fits on
the identical datasets. The \emph{dictionary-size-matched single-scale}
control uses $300$ centres and one validation-selected bandwidth, so it has the
multiscale entry's atom count without its multiple bandwidths. The
\emph{expanded-range} control keeps $60\times5$ atoms and the same centres but
spans $[0.025h,\,8h]$. The expanded range is exploratory and is not promoted
to the main estimator on these data.

\paragraph{Metrics and analysis.} For every fit we saved total, narrow-region
and complement ISE, the selected and final support, the number of active
components ($|w_i| > 10^{-8}\max_j|w_j|$), the minimal storage of an
equivalent representation (three numbers per mixture component; $N$ centres
plus one bandwidth for the Sheather--Jones estimator; $N$ centres plus $N$
bandwidths for Abramson), the serialised file size, tuning and fitting time,
the atom centres, bandwidths and weights, the total weight per dictionary
scale, the number of scales receiving at least $1\%$ of the weight, solver
status, iteration counts, objective values and relative KKT residuals of both
stages, mass error and density validity. Query time was measured afterwards
on every saved model with $10{,}000$ identical points on $[-5,6]$ in batches of
$2{,}000$ after two warm-up calls, taking the median of seven timing repeats
within each model; repeats are not replications. Per cell we report the
arithmetic mean $\pm$ sample SD (ddof $=1$) across the $20$ datasets. Paired
geometric-mean ISE ratios are defined with multiscale AKDE in the numerator, so
values below one favour it, and are accompanied by $95\%$ percentile bootstrap
intervals from $10{,}000$ resamples of dataset identifiers within a cell with
all methods carried together (seed $20260930$). The trend statistic within
each $N$ is the mean paired log-ratio at $r=8$ minus that at $r=1$; a negative
value means a larger relative multiscale benefit at higher heterogeneity.
A large-error fit is one whose ISE exceeds five times the median of its own
(method, $r$, $N$) cell, a rule fixed in advance.

