\subsection{Which bandwidths are selected}
\label{sec:bf-scales}

Figure~\ref{fig:bf-atoms} and Table~\ref{tab:bf-scales} show where the
multiscale fits put their weight. At $N=5000$ the two middle scales,
$0.45h$ and $1.34h$, carry $90$--$97\%$ of the mass in every cell, the
narrowest scale $0.05h$ carries $1$--$5\%$ and $0.15h$ carries $1$--$4\%$; at
$N=500$ the mass sits on $0.45h$ ($68$--$83\%$) and $0.15h$ ($13$--$27\%$), with
at most $1\%$ on $1.34h$. The widest scale $4h$ receives exactly zero weight in
all $160$ AKDE-M fits and all $160$ SPADES-simplex fits. On the rule that a
scale is materially used when it receives at least $1\%$ of the weight, the
fits use $3.2$--$3.8$ of the five scales on average at $N=5000$ and about $3$
at $N=500$, but the narrow scales are used at low weight in every cell,
including the homogeneous target $r=1$, rather than concentrated on the narrow
feature. The single-scale ablation, in contrast, chose its bandwidth in step
with the target: median selected values of
$\bfSelScaleSingleScaleAkdeRoneNSmall h$, $\bfSelScaleSingleScaleAkdeRtwoNSmall h$,
$\bfSelScaleSingleScaleAkdeRfourNSmall h$ and
$\bfSelScaleSingleScaleAkdeReightNSmall h$ at $N=500$ for $r=1,2,4,8$, and
$\bfSelScaleSingleScaleAkdeRoneNLarge h$ (the top of its grid in all $20$
datasets), $\bfSelScaleSingleScaleAkdeRtwoNLarge h$,
$\bfSelScaleSingleScaleAkdeRfourNLarge h$ and
$\bfSelScaleSingleScaleAkdeReightNLarge h$ at $N=5000$, with
$\bfCompNSingleScaleAkdeNSmall$ and $\bfCompNSingleScaleAkdeNLarge$ components
on average against $\bfCompNMultiscaleAkdeNSmall$ and
$\bfCompNMultiscaleAkdeNLarge$ for AKDE-M. The multiscale selector, in other
words, did not use the wide atoms the body of the density calls for, and
assembled the body from several dozen medium and narrow atoms instead; the
single-scale validation search found the wide bandwidth directly. Individual
supports vary from dataset to dataset even where the fitted curves are
similar (Figure~\ref{fig:bf-atoms}, top), which is why the scale-weight
summary over $20$ datasets, not the single example, is the evidence.

