\section{Computational cost and model compactness}
\label{sec:cost}

The computational assessment distinguishes
fitting a model from evaluating the resulting
density. A more expensive fitting procedure
can still be useful when the fitted model
is stored compactly and queried repeatedly.

AKDE-M required seconds per fit, whereas
Sheather--Jones KDE required milliseconds.
Its fitting cost also exceeded that of the
SPADES adaptation. Profiling identified
component selection and constrained refitting
as the main computational tasks.
The phase percentages obtained for a particular
configuration should not be transferred to
another configuration without separate profiling.

For density evaluation, saved models were
queried at $10{,}000$ common locations using
batches of $2{,}000$ points, common warmup
procedures, and one BLAS thread. Seven timing
repeats were performed for each model.
These repeats measure timer variability;
they are not independent fitted datasets.

The reported AKDE-M evaluation times were
$0.99$, $1.12$, and $1.17\,\mu$s per point
at $N=200$, $1000$, and $5000$, respectively.
The corresponding Sheather--Jones times
were $5.17$, $30.3$, and $147.8\,\mu$s.
At $N=5000$, this represents approximately
$127$-fold faster evaluation under the
tested implementation.

The reported serialised model sizes at
$N=5000$ were $1{,}344$ bytes for AKDE-M
and $120{,}000$ bytes for Sheather--Jones KDE,
a ratio of approximately $89$.
These results demonstrate compactness relative
to a full-sample KDE, rather than superiority
over all compact density estimators.

