\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Optimisation accuracy.}
The first-stage solver frequently stopped
before meeting its requested KKT tolerance.
On the diagnostic subset, increasing the
coordinate-descent budget from $500$ to
$10{,}000$ sweeps reduced the median relative
KKT residual from $8.7\times10^{-5}$ to
$1.1\times10^{-6}$. However, the proportion
meeting tolerance increased only from
$13.5\%$ to $24\%$.

The selected supports were identical under
these two budgets in only $18.8\%$ of
diagnostic instances, with a reported median
difference of $21$ atoms. Thus, the numerical
stopping rule influences the implemented
selection procedure.

The second-stage solver generally had smaller
residuals, but occasional failures to reach
tolerance remained. Alternative solvers also
failed to provide uniformly reliable
certificates. The reported confirmation
therefore characterises the deployed numerical
implementation. A solver repair may retain
the mathematical objective while changing
the returned estimates; its practical effects
would require separate evaluation.

\paragraph{Numerical conditioning.}
Some diagnostic matrices had condition
numbers near $10^{19}$. Unconstrained
coefficient solutions were highly sensitive
to numerical rank thresholds, making
distances between computed objective
centres unreliable as mechanistic evidence.
Even constrained oracle calculations require
feasibility and optimality checks; bounded
constraints alone do not guarantee
numerical accuracy.

\paragraph{Experimental scope and precision.}
The study considers univariate densities,
Gaussian dictionaries, one dictionary size,
and $15$ fixed benchmark targets.
The results do not establish performance
for arbitrary density families, multivariate
problems, or other dictionary constructions.

The predefined precision target for per-cell
log-ratio estimates was achieved in $22$
of $45$ target--sample-size conditions.
Consequently, the precision of individual
effect estimates remains uneven.
Mean $\pm$ SD reporting describes observed
variability but does not resolve this
limitation or replace uncertainty analysis.

The measurement functions also depend on
the training sample. Their bandwidths
and locations change with sample size,
even when their number is fixed.
The observed trends therefore do not
establish an asymptotic error floor
or convergence rate.

\paragraph{Multiscale selection.}
The controlled experiment of Section~\ref{sec:bandwidth}
shows that, on a family built to reward it, the five-bandwidth
dictionary is a liability rather than an asset for this
estimator: one cross-validated bandwidth on the same centres
was more accurate in seven of eight cells, and the widest
dictionary scale was never selected. That experiment uses
four targets of one parametric form, two sample sizes and
$20$ datasets per cell, and its single-scale ablation is the
more budget-sensitive of the two AKDE variants under the
deployed first-stage solver. The result bounds what the
dictionary design can be credited with; it does not identify
which of the penalty grid, the selection rule or the solver
is responsible, and it does not transfer to other dictionary
constructions without a fresh confirmation.

\section{Discussion and conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This study evaluates a sparse density estimator
that combines component selection through
moment matching with a constrained refit
ensuring nonnegativity and unit total mass.
Using the same Gaussian functions for
representation and measurement provides
an interpretable design: the population
moment residual equals one half of the
gradient of integrated squared error.

The experiments demonstrate that this
measurement choice substantially affects
estimation accuracy. Matching the two
families reduced paired geometric-mean
ISE relative to the separate-bank ablation
across every tested target and sample size.
However, the oracle diagnostics show that
matching does not eliminate all excess
error, and the resulting estimator remains
less accurate than the conventional
comparators on the benchmark summaries.

The principal practical strength is a compact
representation with fast density evaluation.
At $N=5000$, AKDE-M achieved approximately
$127$-fold faster queries and $89$-fold lower
stored model size than the tested
Sheather--Jones implementation.
This benefit comes with higher fitting
cost and higher estimation error.
Its usefulness therefore depends on the
application's accuracy requirements, storage
constraints, and number of subsequent queries.

The follow-up experiment of Section~\ref{sec:bandwidth}
qualifies the role of the multiscale dictionary.
On two-component targets with a narrow feature,
a single cross-validated bandwidth on the same
centres gave lower error than the five-bandwidth
dictionary in seven of eight cells, a size-matched
single-scale control was better on every paired
dataset, and the widest scale was never used;
SPADES-simplex used the dictionary in the same way.
The estimator's demonstrated strength is therefore
a compact, valid mixture that can be fitted from the
moment criterion and queried cheaply, not a
multiscale representation that captures sharp and
broad features better than its alternatives.

Future work should prioritise
reliable optimisation, the interaction between
penalisation and large-error fits, the selection
rule's apparent preference for narrow atoms over
the wide atoms a density's body calls for, and
sensitivity to dictionary and measurement
design. Extending the approach to
multivariate data will also require
examining how measurement count and
dictionary size affect accuracy and cost.
The present findings establish a measurable
accuracy--compactness trade-off and identify
the numerical and statistical issues that
must be addressed to improve reliability.
